Assignment: Expense Tracker with Summary


Due Date: On or before Monday, April 2, 2025, 9:00 AM
Objective
Build an expense tracker where users can log expenses (e.g., "Lunch", "Transport"), categorize them, and see a summary of total spending by category. This assignment reinforces arrays, objects, iteration, and introduces basic data aggregation.
Requirements
HTML Structure:
A form with:
Text input for expense description (e.g., "Groceries").
Number input for amount (e.g., 500).
Dropdown for category (e.g., "Food", "Travel", "Entertainment", "Other").
A "Add Expense" button.
A list to display all expenses with description, amount, category, and date added.
A summary section showing total spending per category (e.g., "Food: ₦1500").
JavaScript Functionality:


Use let for the expenses array and const for fixed values (e.g., category options).
Store each expense as an object: { description: string, amount: number, category: string, date: Date }.
Write a function to add an expense to the array when the button is clicked (validate that amount > 0 using >).
Display expenses in a list, formatting the date (e.g., "March 28, 2025") and amount (e.g., "₦500").
Create a function to calculate totals per category using array iteration (e.g., forEach or reduce):
Compare category with === to group expenses.
Update the summary section dynamically.
Use string methods (e.g., trim() for description, toFixed(2) for amounts).
Add a "Delete" button next to each expense (use splice or filter).
Include a "Total Expenses" value summing all amounts.
Sort expenses by amount or date (use sort with comparisons).
CSS Basics:
Style the form and list for readability (e.g., grid or flexbox layout).
Color-code categories in the list (e.g., green for "Food", blue for "Travel").
Make the summary section stand out (e.g., bold text, bordered box).



Deliverables
A single HTML file with embedded <script> and <style> tags (or separate files if preferred).
Submit via a link shared in the WhatsApp group.
A short paragraph (3-5 sentences) reflecting on what you learned (e.g., array iteration) or found tricky (e.g., calculating totals).
Tips
Test adding expenses first, then build the summary logic.
Use console.log(expenses) to debug your array.
Check W3Schools/JavasScript.info "JS Array Iteration" for reduce examples.
Learning Goals
Master array iteration and data aggregation.
Practice form handling and input validation.
Strengthen DOM manipulation with dynamic lists and summaries.
Apply number formatting and comparisons for practical use.
